iPod nano 4G flash sequencer (FMSS) programs
============================================

reset.s, readid.s and read.s are the sequencer programs the NAND driver
(../nand-nano4g.c) runs. They are assembled during the build by the host tool
tools/fmssasm (firmware.make includes fmss.make when the target's toolset
lists fmssasm) into $(BUILDDIR)/fmss-nano4g.h, which defines the arrays
fmss_prog_reset, fmss_prog_readid and fmss_prog_read (static, 32-byte
aligned) for nand-nano4g.c. Edit read.s directly.

Programs for the S5L8720 flash sequencer (FMSS). Each instruction is two
words: word0 = arg | reg << 16 | opcode << 24, word1 = immediate.
The instruction set is described below. The sequencer
fetches the program from memory by bus address (FMSS_PROG), so the arrays
must be cache-line aligned and cleaned from the D-cache before use.

Instruction set. Registers r0..r7; r6 is visible to the CPU as
FMSS_SEQ_RESULT (+0xc30) when the program ends. [REG] = MMIO register
0x38a00000 + arg; mem32[rX] = system memory.
  op 00 END   stop, raise CS_IRQ bit 0 (done)
  op 01 WR    [arg] = imm          op 02 ST    [arg] = rR
  op 03 LDI   rR = mem32[r(arg)]   op 04 LD    rR = [arg] & imm
  op 05 MOVI  rR = imm             op 06 MOV   rR = r(arg)
  op 07 WAIT  wait for event R: 0 command sent, 1 address sent, 2 DMA done,
              3 data transferred, 4 ECC done, 12 NAND status match
              (arg = value, mask); a timeout jumps to the fault vector
              (FMSS_C4C, +0xc4c)
  op 0a/0b/0c/0d AND/OR/ADD/SUB, op 13/14 SHL/SHR: imm != 0: rR = r(arg)
              op imm; imm == 0: rR = rR op r(arg)
  op 0e BNZ / op 17 BZ   branch to byte offset imm if rR != 0 / == 0
  op 11 STI   mem32[r(arg)] = rR
Every WAIT is followed by a write of the event's bit to FMC_STATUS
(+0x048, write 1 to clear): bit 1 command, 2 address, 3 data, 20 DMA.

NAND commands issued: 0xFF RESET; 0x90 + 1 address cycle (0x00) + 5 data
bytes = READ ID; 0x00 + 5 address cycles (column 0, 3 row bytes) + 0x30 =
READ PAGE; 0x70 READ STATUS (bit 6 = ready), then 0x00 to return to data
output. ECC: the controller's BCH decoder (ECC_CTRL +0x804: 1 = BCH8,
5 = BCH16, | 0xa00 = sector 0's codeword includes the 10 meta bytes).

Results: RESET and READ ID end with r6 = 0; their
fault handler sets r6 = 0x08000000 | mask of the selected chip enables,
deselects and acknowledges at FMSS_C50 (+0xc50). READ ends with r6 = the
page summary: bit 30 some sector uncorrectable, bit 29 some sector erased,
bit n (0..15) some sector had n bits corrected; its fault handler sets
r6 = summary | 0x08000000. READ also stores, per page, 12 meta bytes
(10 corrected bytes, last word | 0xffff0000), optionally one status byte
per sector (0xff uncorrectable, 0xfe erased, else corrected bits; four
per u32, the first sector in the most significant byte) and an optional
u32[18] histogram (entry n = sectors with n corrections, 17 = uncorrectable).

Sizes: RESET 24 instructions (48 words), READ ID 46 (92 words), READ 552
(1104 words).
